---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
import GuideComparisons from '../../components/GuideComparisons.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const articleJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "Google Perspective API Sunset: Migration Guide for Forums",
  "description": "Perspective API is sunsetting in late 2026. Learn what this means for your forum and how to migrate to OpenModeration before the deadline.",
  "datePublished": "2026-06-12",
  "dateModified": "2026-10-20",
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "OpenModeration", "url": "https://www.openmoderation.com" },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "OpenModeration",
    "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://www.openmoderation.com/logo.svg" }
  },
  "image": "https://www.openmoderation.com/og-image.jpg",
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://www.openmoderation.com/blog/perspective-api-migration-guide" }
};
---

<BaseLayout title="Google Perspective API Sunset: Migration Guide for Forums" description="Perspective API is sunsetting in late 2026. Learn what this means for your forum and how to migrate to OpenModeration before the deadline." jsonLd={articleJsonLd}>
<Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Blog", href: "/blog" }, { label: "Perspective API Migration" }]} />
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 text-sm text-surface-400">
        <time datetime="2026-06-12">June 12, 2026</time>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated October 20, 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>7 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-surface-900 sm:text-4xl">Google Perspective API Sunset: Migration Guide for Forums</h1>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-surface max-w-none text-surface-600">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Jigsaw, the Google unit behind Perspective API, has announced that the service will
        be retired: it remains active until December 31, 2026.
        For the thousands of forums, comment systems, and publishing platforms that rely on it,
        this means finding a Perspective API alternative before the deadline.
      </p>
      <p>
        This guide explains what is changing, what it means for you, and how to move to
        OpenModeration with as little disruption as possible.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What's happening and when</h2>
      <p>
        Perspective API was one of the first widely available toxicity detection APIs. Launched
        by Jigsaw (a Google incubator) in 2017, it powered moderation for platforms like The New
        York Times, Disqus, and countless forum communities.
      </p>
      <p>
        According to the <a href="https://perspectiveapi.com/" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">official Perspective API site</a>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Until February 2026:</strong> requests for increased quota were still handled. No new requests are accepted after that.</li>
        <li><strong>December 31, 2026:</strong> last day of service. The API is no longer available after 2026.</li>
        <li><strong>Migration:</strong> Jigsaw states that it will not offer direct migration support.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Why Perspective API is shutting down</h2>
      <p>
        Jigsaw gives one reason: AI capabilities have evolved, and there is now less demand for
        a standalone tool specific to this area. Indeed, teams can now choose between many
        moderation models, from OpenAI to HuggingFace to Mistral, and most platforms want
        context-aware decisions rather than a single toxicity score.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What this means for your forum</h2>
      <p>
        If you're using Perspective API today, your moderation pipeline will break when the
        shutdown happens. Comments and posts that rely on automated toxicity scoring will go
        unmoderated. You'll see one of three outcomes depending on how deeply you've integrated:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Direct integration:</strong> If your app calls Perspective API directly, requests will fail. Your moderation queue goes dark.</li>
        <li><strong>Through a middleware:</strong> If you use a moderation platform that wraps Perspective, check if they have a migration path.</li>
        <li><strong>Via a social connector:</strong> Some social management tools use Perspective for comment filtering. Verify their plan.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Evaluating Perspective API alternatives</h2>
      <p>
        Here's what to look for in a replacement:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Category coverage:</strong> Perspective detects toxicity, severe toxicity, identity attack, insult, profanity, threat, and sexually explicit. Your replacement should cover these categories and more.</li>
        <li><strong>No frozen model:</strong> Relying on a single vendor's model means you repeat this migration next time they change strategy. Look for a service that upgrades its AI without asking you to change code.</li>
        <li><strong>Latency:</strong> Forum moderation needs to happen before content appears. Aim for sub-second response times.</li>
        <li><strong>Data control:</strong> If you process EU user content, GDPR matters. Look for EU hosting and the ability to export your data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Migration checklist</h2>
      <p>Follow these steps to migrate off Perspective API:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Audit your current usage.</strong> Count daily API calls, identify which categories you use, and measure your latency requirements. This gives you a baseline for the new setup.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose a replacement architecture.</strong> A moderation platform like OpenModeration keeps your integration stable while the AI behind it improves.</li>
        <li><strong>Map categories.</strong> Map your existing Perspective categories to the equivalent in your new provider. For example, Perspective's TOXICITY maps to categories like hate and harassment in most modern systems.</li>
        <li><strong>Retune thresholds.</strong> Different providers score differently. Run both systems in parallel for a week to calibrate your thresholds.</li>
        <li><strong>Update your integration.</strong> Replace your Perspective API call with the new API endpoint. With OpenModeration, this is a single POST /v1/moderation call.</li>
        <li><strong>Cut over.</strong> Point production traffic to the new system. Keep Perspective as a fallback for the transition period.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Migrating to OpenModeration</h2>
      <p>
        OpenModeration is the most straightforward Perspective API migration path. It provides
        a single API backed by its own moderation AI, with delicate cases cross-checked by other
        AI models. When a better model comes out, we plug it in without any code change on your side.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here's what a migration looks like with OpenModeration:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Before (Perspective API):</strong> Your code makes a direct call to
        commentanalyzer.googleapis.com with a specific API key. You get back attribute scores.
        You hard-code thresholds. Changing vendors means rewriting this integration.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>After (OpenModeration):</strong> Your code calls POST /v1/moderation with
        the content text. OpenModeration analyses it with its own moderation AI, cross-checks
        delicate cases, and returns a consistent format. When we upgrade the AI, no code
        changes are needed.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">What you gain beyond the migration</h2>
      <p>
        Migrating away from Perspective API is a forced upgrade. OpenModeration gives you
        capabilities that Perspective never had:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Action Engine:</strong> Define rules with three tools (forbidden words, thresholds and your own rules in plain language) without writing custom code.</li>
        <li><strong>Audit trail:</strong> Every moderation decision is logged with full context. Exportable for compliance reporting.</li>
        <li><strong>Analytics:</strong> Dashboard showing moderation volume, flagged rates, actions taken, and category breakdowns.</li>
        <li><strong>DSA readiness:</strong> The audit logs and transparency data you need for EU Digital Services Act compliance.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-surface-900">Timeline and next steps</h2>
      <p>
        Google's Perspective API sunset deadline is end of 2026. The earlier you start your
        Perspective API migration, the more time you have to calibrate thresholds and test
        in production. We recommend beginning the process at least 90 days before your target
        cutover date.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a complete guide to setting up OpenModeration, visit our documentation. Your
        first integration takes about 30 minutes.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="border-t border-surface-100 bg-surface-50 py-12">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-surface-900">Keep exploring</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/content-moderation-api" class="font-medium text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-500">One content moderation API for your migration &rarr;</a></li>
